I'll solve this directly in the TUI using the standard integral definitions of the Fourier sine and cosine transforms.

---

## Problem

Compute
$$F_s\!\left(\frac{x}{x^2+c^2}\right) \quad\text{and}\quad F_c\!\left(\frac{1}{x^2+c^2}\right),$$
where, with the convention
$$F_c\{f\}(s)=\int_0^\infty f(x)\cos(sx)\,dx,\qquad F_s\{f\}(s)=\int_0^\infty f(x)\sin(sx)\,dx,$$
and $c>0$, $s>0$.

---

## Step 1 — The cosine transform of $\dfrac{1}{x^2+c^2}$

Consider the contour integral
$$I=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{e^{isx}}{x^2+c^2}\,dx,\qquad s>0.$$
Since $x^2+c^2=(x-ic)(x+ic)$, the only pole in the **upper** half-plane (where we close the contour for $s>0$, since $e^{isx}=e^{is(\Re x+i\Im x)}$ decays as $\Im x\to+\infty$) is at $x=ic$.

The residue there is
$$\operatorname{Res}_{x=ic}\frac{e^{isx}}{x^2+c^2}=\frac{e^{is(ic)}}{2ic}=\frac{e^{-cs}}{2ic}.$$

By the residue theorem,
$$I=2\pi i\cdot\frac{e^{-cs}}{2ic}=\frac{\pi}{c}\,e^{-cs}.$$

Taking the real part (the imaginary part vanishes by symmetry since $1/(x^2+c^2)$ is even):
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\cos(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{c}\,e^{-cs}.$$

The integrand is even in $x$, so
$$\boxed{\;F_c\!\left\{\frac{1}{x^2+c^2}\right\}(s)=\int_0^\infty\frac{\cos(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{2c}\,e^{-cs}.\;}$$

---

## Step 2 — The sine transform of $\dfrac{x}{x^2+c^2}$

Use the standard derivative identity for Fourier cosine transforms:
$$\frac{d}{ds}F_c\{f\}(s)=\frac{d}{ds}\int_0^\infty f(x)\cos(sx)\,dx=-\int_0^\infty x\,f(x)\sin(sx)\,dx=-F_s\{x\,f(x)\}(s).$$

Hence
$$F_s\{x\,f(x)\}(s)=-\frac{d}{ds}F_c\{f\}(s).$$

Set $f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x^2+c^2}$. Using the result of Step 1,
$$-\frac{d}{ds}\left[\frac{\pi}{2c}\,e^{-cs}\right]=-\frac{\pi}{2c}\cdot(-c)\,e^{-cs}=\frac{\pi}{2}\,e^{-cs}.$$

Therefore
$$\boxed{\;F_s\!\left\{\frac{x}{x^2+c^2}\right\}(s)=\int_0^\infty\frac{x\sin(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{2}\,e^{-cs}.\;}$$

---

## Step 3 — Direct verification of the sine result (independent check)

Consider
$$J=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{x\,e^{isx}}{x^2+c^2}\,dx,\qquad s>0.$$
Close in the upper half-plane; the pole at $x=ic$ gives
$$\operatorname{Res}_{x=ic}\frac{x\,e^{isx}}{x^2+c^2}=\frac{ic\cdot e^{-cs}}{2ic}=\frac{e^{-cs}}{2}.$$
Thus
$$J=2\pi i\cdot\frac{e^{-cs}}{2}=i\pi\,e^{-cs}.$$
Now $x\cos(sx)/(x^2+c^2)$ is odd, so its integral over $\mathbb R$ vanishes; $x\sin(sx)/(x^2+c^2)$ is even. Hence
$$J=i\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{x\sin(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx=i\cdot 2\int_0^\infty\frac{x\sin(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx.$$
Equating: $i\cdot 2\int_0^\infty\frac{x\sin(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx=i\pi e^{-cs}$, giving
$$\int_0^\infty\frac{x\sin(sx)}{x^2+c^2}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{2}\,e^{-cs},$$
confirming Step 2.

---

## Step 4 — Symmetric (unitary) normalization variant

If one adopts the symmetric convention
$$\mathcal F_c\{f\}(s)=\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}\int_0^\infty f(x)\cos(sx)\,dx,\qquad \mathcal F_s\{f\}(s)=\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}\int_0^\infty f(x)\sin(sx)\,dx,$$
the answers become
$$\mathcal F_c\!\left\{\frac{1}{x^2+c^2}\right\}(s)=\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\,\frac{e^{-cs}}{c},\qquad \mathcal F_s\!\left\{\frac{x}{x^2+c^2}\right\}(s)=\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\,e^{-cs}.$$

---

## Final answers (integral convention, $c>0$, $s>0$)

$$F_s\!\left(\frac{x}{x^2+c^2}\right)=\frac{\pi}{2}\,e^{-cs},\qquad F_c\!\left(\frac{1}{x^2+c^2}\right)=\frac{\pi}{2c}\,e^{-cs}.$$

Note the elegant relation $F_s\!\left\{\dfrac{x}{x^2+c^2}\right\}=c\cdot F_c\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{x^2+c^2}\right\}$, which is exactly the derivative identity $F_s\{x f\}=-\tfrac{d}{ds}F_c\{f\}$ applied to $f=1/(x^2+c^2)$.

